000FAC  2               ; print_bitmap_line: prints a $FF-terminated string of glyph codes (see
000FAC  2               ; the letter/digit code scheme above str_scr below) as MULTICOLOR bitmap
000FAC  2               ; glyphs at true BBC letter pitch (measured from the reference: gap is
000FAC  2               ; ~25% of glyph width, i.e. ~1 color-pixel for a 5-wide glyph - not the
000FAC  2               ; 3-pixel gap a naive 2-full-cells-per-letter layout gives). temp2 =
000FAC  2               ; source string ptr, temp3 = dest bitmap ptr (the cell containing this
000FAC  2               ; letter's first color-pixel), temp4 = matching dest screen-matrix ptr,
000FAC  2               ; colour = plain 0-15 foreground value.
000FAC  2               ;
000FAC  2               ; A 6-color-pixel advance for a 5-wide glyph (1px gap) doesn't land on
000FAC  2               ; cell boundaries (cells are 4 color-pixels wide), so consecutive
000FAC  2               ; letters alternate which color-pixel offset they start at within a
000FAC  2               ; cell - 0, then 2, then 0, then 2... (6 mod 4 = 2). At offset 2, a
000FAC  2               ; letter's first 2 color-pixels land in the SAME cell the previous
000FAC  2               ; letter's last color-pixel used, so that shared cell's byte is built
000FAC  2               ; from both letters' contributions via ORA, not overwritten via STA -
000FAC  2               ; each letter only ever sets new bits (background is 0), never clears
000FAC  2               ; the other's. temp1 tracks the alternating state (0/1) across the
000FAC  2               ; whole string, reset to 0 (offset 0) at the start of each call.
000FAC  2               print_bitmap_line:
000FAC  2  A9 00            lda #0
000FAE  2  85 rr            sta temp1            ; 0 = next letter at offset 0, 1 = offset 2
000FB0  2               pbl_char:
000FB0  2  A0 00            ldy #0
000FB2  2  B1 rr            lda (temp2),y
